It's slightly more complicated than that, but basically you're usually
right. There are scripts, going back to the Romans, which only have the
round s. There are also scripts going back to the half-uncial with only
the long s. There are scripts with both back to the Carolingians and
Irish monks.
Obviously, if there's one kind of s, that's the one used. If a script
has both, though, thigns can get muddled. Sometimes, both are used
wherever it pleased the scribe. If there _is_ a regular pattern, though,
it's always that the round s is used to end a word; that the long s is
prefered before low letters; and that if there are two esses, they're
either both long, both round, or long followed by round, and the latter
only if they belong to the same syllable. These rules are frequently
broken, but rarely reversed: e.g., long s can be used to end a word, but
is hardly ever prefered there.
And yes, there is a long s in the copperplate hand much of Europe used
in the 17th to early 20th century, but it seems to have fallen into
disuse by the time of the Victorians. Not in Germany, though - I have a
little booklet here printed in 1923, in German black letter
(Schwabacher, I think), which follows the above rules to the letter.
Hence also the German double s, which is nothing but a ligatured long
and round s.

